1. `CanonicalActorJoinRequest_HandoverLeavesReplyRouteToCore` — the replacement submits `ReplyJoin(Ok)` on the target ingress
   of a new request, but the caller ends TimedOut(101) (`stage2-dotnet-mesh-unwind-summary.md` B3). Stage 1: trace which pair
   the reply is pinned to vs which pair the caller's request used (Core pins the reply to the submit-time pair; after the
   unwind the framework no longer re-pins) — is the framework replying to the wrong correlation/route, is the test's raw
   fixture replying on a pipe Core superseded (then the request should have completed NOT_CONNECTED immediately — check that
   completion arrives; if it does not, that is a Core defect → STOP and report), or does the test assert a behaviour the amended
   spec no longer defines (then it is the test that encodes a removed rule)? Classify A/B/C/D; minimal fix.
2. `ManagedSource_Command28Request_Retries_Same_Correlation_After_Lost_Reply` — the fixture withholds the first reply while the
   pair stays alive and expects a second ingress within 7 s of an 8 s operation; that relied on the removed fixed 5 s attempt
   cut. Under the sender policy a withheld reply on a live pair cannot be detected before the deadline (admitted → no reply →
   DeadlineExceeded); a reply is "lost" in practice because the pair terminates, which Core now reports as NOT_CONNECTED. Rewrite
   the fixture to the spec: simulate the lost reply as a pair termination (the losing HANDOVER direction, or the raw peer
   closing the pipe) so Core completes the first attempt NOT_CONNECTED and the sender replays with the same OperationId/
   correlation within the original deadline; keep the assertion "same correlation, exactly one execution". Class B (fixture
   encodes a removed rule) unless stage 1 shows otherwise.
